  questions: [
    { q: "The THEME of a literary work is BEST described as:", o: ["The subject matter or topic the story is about", "The central message or insight about human life that the work conveys", "The sequence of events from beginning to end", "The time and place in which the story is set"], a: 1, e: "Theme is the central message, insight, or statement about human life that emerges from the work — not merely the subject (topic). 'War' is a subject; 'War destroys what it claims to protect' is a theme. Themes are typically expressed as complete statements, not single words.", h: "Theme = STATEMENT/MESSAGE about human life (not just the topic). 'Love' = subject. 'Love demands sacrifice' = theme. Full statement, not a word.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "In Freytag's Pyramid of plot structure, the CLIMAX is:", o: ["The opening of the story where characters are introduced", "The falling action after the main conflict is resolved", "The moment of highest tension or the turning point of the plot", "The final outcome where loose ends are tied up"], a: 2, e: "The climax is the peak of the pyramid — the moment of greatest tension and the turning point after which the story's direction changes. Everything in the rising action leads to the climax; everything after it (falling action, denouement) flows from it.", h: "Climax = PEAK of pyramid = highest tension + TURNING POINT. Everything before leads to it; everything after flows from it.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The SETTING of a literary work includes:", o: ["Only the geographical location (country, city, village)", "Time, place, and social environment in which the story takes place", "The emotional atmosphere created for the reader", "The author's attitude toward the characters"], a: 1, e: "Setting encompasses: PHYSICAL setting (place/location), TEMPORAL setting (time period, season, time of day), and SOCIAL setting (cultural, economic, and political environment). Setting actively influences characters, drives plot, creates atmosphere, and supports theme.", h: "Setting = TIME + PLACE + SOCIAL ENVIRONMENT. Three dimensions: physical (where), temporal (when), social (what kind of society).", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The TONE of a literary work refers to:", o: ["The emotional atmosphere experienced by the reader", "The author's emotional attitude toward the subject matter, conveyed through language", "The pace at which the plot moves", "The geographical and temporal context of the story"], a: 1, e: "Tone is the author's emotional attitude toward the subject — whether sarcastic, celebratory, melancholic, angry, sympathetic, etc. It is conveyed through word choice, imagery, and narrative commentary. Tone is distinct from mood (the reader's emotional experience).", h: "Tone = AUTHOR'S attitude toward subject. How the AUTHOR feels about what they write. Mood = READER's emotional experience. Different directions.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The DENOUEMENT of a plot is:", o: ["The moment of highest tension and conflict", "The section where events build toward the climax", "The final resolution — where loose ends are tied up after the climax", "The introduction of characters and setting at the beginning"], a: 2, e: "Denouement (French: 'unknotting') is the final part of the plot — after the climax and falling action — where the conflict is resolved, questions are answered, and the narrative is brought to a close. It is the untying of the story's narrative 'knots.'", h: "Denouement = FINAL RESOLUTION. After climax and falling action. Conflict resolved, story closed. French: 'unknotting' = untying plot's knots.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "MOOD in literary appreciation is defined as:", o: ["The author's emotional attitude toward the subject matter", "The emotional atmosphere that the text creates in the reader", "The central message or theme of the work", "The setting's influence on the characters' behavior"], a: 1, e: "Mood (also called atmosphere) is the emotional feeling that the text creates in the reader — how the text makes you feel as you read. Common moods: tense, peaceful, melancholic, terrifying, hopeful, romantic. Mood differs from tone (the author's attitude).", h: "Mood = reader's EMOTIONAL EXPERIENCE (how text makes YOU feel). Tone = AUTHOR'S attitude. Mood is IN the reader; tone is IN the author.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "An author's STYLE refers to:", o: ["The plot structure they use in their work", "Their distinctive manner of writing — word choice, sentence structure, voice, imagery, and technique", "The themes they typically explore in their works", "The setting they most commonly use in their stories"], a: 1, e: "Style is the author's distinctive fingerprint — the sum of all their choices about language and technique: diction (word choice), sentence structure, narrative voice, use of figurative language, pace, and more. Each author's style is recognizable and different from others.", h: "Style = AUTHOR'S DISTINCTIVE WAY of writing. Sum of choices: diction, sentence structure, voice, imagery, pace. Makes one author recognizable from another.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Which of the following correctly identifies SUBJECT vs. THEME?", o: ["Subject: 'Power corrupts leaders who forget their people.' Theme: 'Power'", "Subject: 'Love.' Theme: 'True love demands great sacrifice and courage.'", "Subject: 'This novel is set in colonial Nigeria.' Theme: 'Nigeria'", "Subject and theme are the same thing in literary analysis"], a: 1, e: "'Love' (subject — what the story is about) vs. 'True love demands great sacrifice and courage' (theme — what the author says about love). Theme is always a STATEMENT about the subject, expressing the author's insight or message.", h: "Subject = TOPIC (one word). Theme = STATEMENT about the topic ('Love demands sacrifice'). Examiners penalize subject words given as themes.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The EXPOSITION in a story's plot structure serves to:", o: ["Build tension toward the story's turning point", "Introduce characters, setting, and the initial situation before conflict develops", "Resolve the central conflict of the story", "Provide the highest point of dramatic tension"], a: 1, e: "The exposition is the opening phase of the plot — it establishes the world of the story by introducing key characters, the setting (time and place), and the initial situation or background. It gives the reader the context needed to understand the conflict when it develops.", h: "Exposition = OPENING: introduces CHARACTERS, SETTING, and initial SITUATION. 'The world before the conflict.' Foundation for everything that follows.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "A FLASHBACK in plot technique is when:", o: ["The narrator predicts future events in the story", "The story begins in the middle of action (in medias res)", "The narrative goes back in time to show events that occurred before the story's present", "Two storylines alternate throughout the narrative"], a: 2, e: "A flashback is a narrative device where the story jumps back in time to show events that happened before the current narrative moment. Flashbacks reveal background information, explain characters' motivations, and add depth to the plot without telling the story chronologically.", h: "Flashback = narrative GOES BACK IN TIME to show earlier events. Reveals background/character motivation. Contrast: foreshadowing (hints of FUTURE events).", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "A story set in a dark, decaying, abandoned house with constant rain creates which MOOD?", o: ["Joyful and celebratory", "Peaceful and serene", "Gloomy, oppressive, and foreboding", "Romantic and tender"], a: 2, e: "The combination of physical elements — dark, decaying setting + constant rain + abandonment — creates a gloomy, oppressive, and foreboding mood. Setting and weather are primary tools for creating mood in fiction. The reader unconsciously absorbs these signals and feels the intended atmosphere.", h: "Dark decaying house + rain = GLOOMY/OPPRESSIVE/FOREBODING mood. Setting creates mood through sensory details and associations.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The RISING ACTION in Freytag's Pyramid consists of:", o: ["The events after the climax that lead to resolution", "The introduction of the main characters and setting", "The events that develop the conflict and build tension toward the climax", "The final resolution of the main conflict"], a: 2, e: "Rising action is the section between the exposition and the climax — the events that complicate the conflict, develop tension, and drive the narrative toward the critical moment of the climax. Without strong rising action, the climax lacks impact.", h: "Rising action = events BETWEEN exposition and climax. Develop conflict, BUILD TENSION. Everything driving TOWARD the climax.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "When an author uses short, punchy sentences and simple vocabulary throughout a novel, this is an aspect of the author's:", o: ["Theme", "Mood", "Setting", "Style"], a: 3, e: "Sentence structure choices (short, punchy sentences vs. long, complex ones) and vocabulary choices (simple vs. complex) are elements of the author's STYLE — their distinctive manner of writing. Ernest Hemingway's famously short sentences are a recognizable style element.", h: "Short punchy sentences + simple vocabulary = choices about STYLE (how the author writes). Style = sum of all writing technique choices.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "A story told in the THIRD PERSON OMNISCIENT narrative voice means:", o: ["The story is told from the perspective of a character using 'I'", "The narrator knows everything about all characters — their thoughts, feelings, and motivations", "The narrator only knows what one character knows and feels", "The story is addressed directly to 'you' the reader"], a: 1, e: "Third person omniscient ('omniscient' = all-knowing): the narrator uses 'he/she/they' and can access the inner thoughts, feelings, and motivations of all characters. This contrasts with third person limited (only one character's inner life), first person (I), and second person (you).", h: "Third person omniscient: narrator is ALL-KNOWING (can enter ANY character's mind). 'He thought...', 'She felt...' for ANY character in the story.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "FORESHADOWING as a plot technique involves:", o: ["Revealing past events through a jump back in time", "The resolution of the main conflict at the end", "Hints or clues earlier in the narrative about events that will happen later", "The use of symbols to represent abstract ideas"], a: 2, e: "Foreshadowing is a narrative technique where the author plants hints, clues, or omens earlier in the story about events that will occur later. It creates anticipation, makes the plot feel inevitable, and rewards careful readers who notice the early signals.", h: "Foreshadowing = EARLY HINTS/CLUES about FUTURE events. Creates anticipation, makes plot feel inevitable. Contrast: flashback (past events).", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The SOCIAL SETTING of a story refers to:", o: ["The physical landscape and geography where events occur", "The cultural, economic, class, and political environment in which the story takes place", "The time period (historical era) when the story is set", "The emotional atmosphere created by the setting"], a: 1, e: "Social setting encompasses the cultural context, class structures, economic conditions, political systems, and social norms of the world in which the story takes place. In Things Fall Apart, the social setting — Igbo traditional society with its clan structures — is crucial to understanding the characters and conflicts.", h: "Social setting = CULTURAL, ECONOMIC, CLASS, and POLITICAL environment. Not just physical place — the SOCIETY and its structures.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "When a novel's narrator seems detached and objective, reporting events without commenting on them emotionally, this indicates a __ tone.", o: ["Passionate", "Melancholic", "Ironic", "Detached/Neutral"], a: 3, e: "A detached or neutral tone means the author (through the narrator) maintains emotional distance and objectivity — reporting events without explicit emotional commentary or editorializing. Hemingway's journalism-influenced style often uses this tone. It contrasts with passionate, melancholic, or ironic tones.", h: "Detached/neutral tone: author DOES NOT EDITORIALIZE or show emotion. Reports events objectively. Journalism-influenced literary style.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The SUBPLOT of a story is:", o: ["The main storyline followed by the protagonist", "A secondary storyline that runs alongside the main plot, often involving minor characters", "An alternative ending proposed by the author", "A flashback sequence that reveals past events"], a: 1, e: "A subplot is a secondary narrative thread that runs alongside the main plot — it typically involves minor characters but may connect thematically to the main plot. Subplots add depth, provide contrast, and may comment on the themes of the main plot.", h: "Subplot = SECONDARY storyline alongside main plot. Often involves MINOR characters. May connect thematically. Adds depth and contrast.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "An author who writes about political corruption with bitter sarcasm demonstrates which TONE?", o: ["Sympathetic and gentle", "Celebratory", "Satirical and bitter", "Romantic and hopeful"], a: 2, e: "Bitter sarcasm when writing about political corruption reveals a SATIRICAL tone (using irony and humor to expose and criticize) combined with BITTERNESS (deep disappointment and anger). Satirical writing attacks its target through irony, exaggeration, and contemptuous humor.", h: "Bitter sarcasm about corruption = SATIRICAL + BITTER tone. Satire = using irony/humor to CRITICIZE. Bitterness = deep anger at the situation.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Literary appreciation requires a reader to:", o: ["Simply enjoy the story without analyzing it", "Read quickly to cover as many books as possible", "Thoughtfully analyze HOW and WHY the text creates meaning and effect", "Memorize the plot and characters without evaluating the writing"], a: 2, e: "Literary appreciation goes beyond passive enjoyment — it involves active, thoughtful analysis of HOW the text achieves its effects (through style, structure, language) and WHY these choices are effective. It asks not just 'what happens' but 'how is this written and why does it work this way?'", h: "Literary appreciation = ACTIVE ANALYSIS of HOW and WHY text creates effects. Not just enjoying or memorizing — ANALYZING technique and meaning.", yr: "SS1" }
  ]
